This code is a simple implementation of the requirements. It uses the `gpiod` and `spi0` libraries for GPIO and SPI operations, respectively. The `spi0` library is not part of the standard Linux distribution and needs to be compiled and installed separately.

The `spi_read_max31855` function is also not part of the `spi0` library and needs to be implemented separately. It should read a temperature from a MAX31855 thermocouple amplifier connected to the specified SPI device and CS GPIO.

The `spi_init`, `spi_open`, `gpiod_chip_open`, `gpiod_chip_get_line`, `gpiod_line_request_pwm_output`, `gpiod_line_set_pwm_duty_cycle`, and `gpiod_line_set_value` functions all return a status code that should be checked after calling them. In this code, we exit the program if any of these calls fail.

The `sigint_handler` function is registered to handle the SIGINT signal (usually sent by pressing Ctrl+C). It stops the fan, closes the SPI device, releases the GPIO line, closes the GPIO chip, prints the contents of the circular buffer, and then exits the program.

The main loop reads the temperature, computes the fan duty cycle, stores the current timestamp, temperature, and duty cycle in the circular buffer, and then sets the fan duty cycle. If the SPI read fails, it prints an error message and skips the fan update for that cycle.

